#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166550 :question/id #uuid "8337af7a-73e8-5ae2-9bed-b03f193b2950" :question/math-academy-id "e-21657" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1 {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sin   ( 3 t ) \\\\ 3 \\cos   ( 3 t )\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\cos   ( 3 t ) \\\\ 3 \\sin   ( 3 t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R},\n$$\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above." :question/worked-solution "We are given that the general solution of the system is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1 {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sin   ( 3 t ) \\\\ 3 \\cos   ( 3 t )\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\cos   ( 3 t ) \\\\ 3 \\sin   ( 3 t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nFor example, when $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ y &=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.71$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/3c/3c2ea4467854f749e07f5ae3922273a2ebd441da4fb9df834ce0676157e856a2.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(3t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(3t)| \\leq 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is bounded. So, we have an ellipse that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df8e7ae5f7ff7281cbdbf5849dc94faf59d2421621dc5009b21eeb947485d153.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166551 :question/id #uuid "7b4f569b-ad3a-5a93-a53e-27a186b7700e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21658" :question/problem "The phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda = a \\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Provide an interpretation of the portrait and determine the possible values of $a.$\n\n![](images/6d/6d13d0dfadac45d40b74cb7e740a880305098f0939e2f45d74706c4022183c54.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is unbounded, representing a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise. Thus, we must have $a \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward the origin.\n\nHence, to insure that $e^{at} \\to 0,$ we require $a < 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166552 :question/id #uuid "d7b40689-7b7e-546e-9b66-30e6a95f3ee0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21659" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda=\\pm2\\text{i}.$ Provide an interpretation of the equilibrium point (origin) for the system of differential equations given above." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a = \\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0$ and $e^{at} = e^{0\\cdot t} = 1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is a center."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "931c80f3-d825-589f-b2ef-dcfd7dda5e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\cos (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164403} {:answer/id #uuid "9d745451-32e5-56f5-873e-5d44bd359783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164404} {:answer/id #uuid "c7476285-a61e-526b-9239-dbfa7e20ad68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sin (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164405} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7038c7-076d-5f7d-8904-412d4024c10c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sin (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164406}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6d633e3b-d76d-5046-9677-2e813d21d36a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164402} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9b19b13-8e85-5cca-9303-b0250b36e640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164408} {:answer/id #uuid "d626414d-c8aa-5082-ac3a-5c14e336a24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin (6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218616440