db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305819} {:answer/id #uuid "567b7694-1344-56c8-87b8-952e8332ee9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305988} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce7ad7c-852a-588c-b00f-a6c8988c21d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43faaa59-8199-5681-a6f1-14e5c433b536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305819} :answer-field/id #uuid "be2d7290-add1-5764-9c49-5badc0c90d22" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308118} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb0afe84-70c9-5685-b972-84cf5eb0124b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ellipse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306892} {:answer/id #uuid "fce2c5cc-8978-5d44-8d5b-312ffa6477ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a spiral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fce2c5cc-8978-5d44-8d5b-312ffa6477ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a spiral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307487} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce292717-4d39-52e1-a536-6e10a4c8b2cd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a0b7f27-9af8-5e95-9ef4-9450271cfa1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334279" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4be4e2001c6c2f499ea95b932ba600772827ca5ce4df2abedb629ac5d4daee75.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-5}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\nHence, \n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to 0,$ we require $a<0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd47a1a-8da1-50b6-a767-dc9eb92e7d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "unbounded" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305433} {:answer/id #uuid "5c76fbdb-fc