nsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-1}} \\\\ y &={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.05$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1.05$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4b/4bcb5580c38573cfdd9839a9d305deec877945a2206aea7db6d69ec37d20ee33.png)\n\nAs $t\\to \\infty ,$ the curve\n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-8}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/07/07d13106e7599d36636da2101ac0bc8fb73b26062416f915a0253097eb85108c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ y &={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.05$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1.05$ | $2.19$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4b/4bcb5580c38573cfdd9839a9d305deec877945a2206aea7db6d69ec37d20ee33.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)|\\le 1$ and $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)|\\le 1$ but ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{diverges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/07/07d13106e7599d36636da2101ac0bc8fb73b26062416f915a0253097eb85108c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a177b1d-8f4d-5ce4-96d5-0641118ffbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427001} {:answer/id #uuid "44d3d3bc-85e9-5891-a136-208e1a7ae81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427009} {:answer/id #uuid "59791c19-fe10-5a49-8c0d-1483606ff223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427016} {:answer/id #uuid "61bacac7-6dd5-50c2-956e-a48c2f0f54d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427020} {:answer/id #uuid "6a53884e-4438-5bc3-ba6b-7c3120bf76ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427027} {:answer/id #uuid "fd467763-82ff-5759-bf17-4bb9e315a854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d3d3bc-85e9-5891-a136-208e1a7ae81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427009} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d312be2-8e22-53a7-b0ac-f747e3a9aeda" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f18f9e5-125e-52fa-a4bf-f9023b9d06e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427013} {:answer/id #uuid "8c413faf-a70e-5b39-b44f-b5ca5bb38ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427041} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c8c4d4-3353-5b89-8ca9-42cba1be9033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6c8c4d4-3353-5b89-8ca9-42cba1be9033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427092} :answer-field/id #uuid "73156d6c-fd43-56c5-bed5-47e0532e4a14" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c1fcfe84-fe11-5391-abf0-f5881c1a0ef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334305" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t)-8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=8x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =5\\pm 8\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=5>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64d15c0d-286c-5db2-a374-6e5e4f0ca2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427023} {:answer/id #uuid "8f04802d-784a-549b-887a-7dbd17012ce3" :ans