\\infty ,$ the curve\n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-8}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f60bdf0ec9c734a1d0d352c448b2c829869ba9fe0115bd860f639b9e4dc83cd0.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.86$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.86$ | $1.48$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/50/50b71bd4aefb94179b55a94992f9e7a42b9ddbd50cd3a9dcf17645f4afd4846c.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)|\\le 1$ and $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)|\\le 1$ but ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{diverges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f60bdf0ec9c734a1d0d352c448b2c829869ba9fe0115bd860f639b9e4dc83cd0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb8140c-7d21-5826-b8c6-8681a1f60f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533282} {:answer/id #uuid "c11c5670-2897-5382-a387-a92972ba5b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533606} {:answer/id #uuid "cba3c1d8-60c5-5ce0-9eb7-93b5c36fab46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cba3c1d8-60c5-5ce0-9eb7-93b5c36fab46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533630} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ecaf0db-258e-5d47-8f5d-62e1aab6739b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533831} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d50709-aa76-582b-9410-59b11f93d7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533196} {:answer/id #uuid "227a7a14-1e6b-5339-aa40-b425a6caeddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533223} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf21272-b21f-5d98-9834-5ad7916a9c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533324} {:answer/id #uuid "620c9ad3-6c3f-5474-acac-ef780aed0dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533364} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd13b7f-f0ef-539c-8da4-07661c578421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533634} {:answer/id #uuid "d292b233-658f-5ac4-a192-6e5e28d2210a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "227a7a14-1e6b-5339-aa40-b425a6caeddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533223} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf45c67d-750d-505d-989a-2337e3e5243c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cbeb2c34-b611-5622-babc-25dc1311116c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334309" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-3<0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable spiral (sink)}.$"}]}
